The excerpts only partly answer this. The closest option is a `startup` event handler, which runs *before* the app begins receiving requests, not after it starts [1]:

```python
from fastapi import FastAPI

app = FastAPI()

items = {}

@app.on_event("startup")
async def startup_event():
    items["foo"] = {"name": "Fighters"}
    items["bar"] = {"name": "Tenders"}
```

You can add more than one startup handler, and the app won't receive requests until all of them have completed [1]. The section is labeled "Alternative Events (deprecated)" [1].

The excerpts don't describe any way to run code after startup has finished, and they don't explain the newer "Lifespan Events" approach that the section heading refers to [1].

The excerpts don't describe a way to show one docs page covering all mounted sub-applications. They describe the opposite setup: each app has its own docs. The main app's docs at `/docs` include only its own _path operations_, and the sub-application's docs at `/subapi/docs` include only its own, under the `/subapi` prefix [1]. The sub-application is meant to have "its own independent OpenAPI and their own docs UIs" [2].

The excerpts show this mount pattern:

```python
from fastapi import FastAPI

app = FastAPI()

@app.get("/app")
def read_main():
    return {"message": "Hello World from main app"}

subapi = FastAPI()

@subapi.get("/sub")
def read_sub():
    return {"message": "Hello World from sub API"}

app.mount("/subapi", subapi)
```

[3][4][5]

FastAPI passes the mount path to the sub-application through a `root_path` so its docs UI uses the correct prefix [6]. The excerpts don't say whether any combined documentation view is possible.
